Clockwork Meridian

Creative Catalyst: Instruments forged from fallen star-iron and salvaged clockwork gear-trains that physically tick while they scream.

This genre is a jagged, rhythmic exorcism. It subverts Chicago Blues by replacing the soulful swing with rigid, frantic 16th-note syncopations; it strips Ska of its upbeat optimism, turning it into a nervous, mechanical twitch; and it deconstructs the polyrhythms of Bomba, Plena, and Merengue into a claustrophobic, metallic clatter that feels like an engine room operating under extreme duress.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 145–160 BPM; a relentless, forward-leaning mechanical pulse that feels like a skipped heartbeat.
*   Harmonic Palette: Diminished blues scales layered over Caribbean montuno patterns, played in non-tempered microtonal tuning.
*   Production Signatures: "Gear-Shift Gating" (the sound is chopped by the physical ticking of the clockwork components), "Star-Dust Saturation" (high-frequency grit generated by iron-ore oscillators), and "Reverb-Compression" (room mics that only record the echoes within a sealed metal chamber).
*   Dynamics: Start-stop brutality; silence is as much an instrument as the sound, punctuated by abrupt, screeching peaks.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the subterranean "Foundry District" of a city that forgot the sun, this movement emerged when laborers began soldering meteorite fragments into old horological parts to pass the crushing hours of a graveyard shift. The fashion is defined by heavy, soot-stained clockwork corsets and translucent aprons, worn by practitioners who treat every performance as a sacred, dangerous maintenance ritual. They believe the universe is a dying machine, and their music is the only way to re-lubricate the gears of reality before the final, permanent freeze.

Instruments: Meteorite-slide guitars, clockwork-geared brass horns, hammered-star metal percussion, and pneumatic bellows.

For Fans Of: Tom Waits, The Skatalites (dismantled), early industrial Einstürzende Neubauten, Eddie Palmieri (at maximum velocity).

